[简体中文](README.md) · **English**

# dsh-webview · Web panel

Open [chat.deepseek.com](https://chat.deepseek.com) right inside DSH Desktop — no extra browser window, no repeated logins.

> 🤖 **Entirely AI-generated**: the code, docs and icon were produced by an AI agent inside DSH, not written line-by-line by a human.

---

## Three entry points, one page

| Entry | Where | Best for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Conversation view tab** | A 网页端 tab next to 对话 / 轨迹 / 上下文 | Working alongside the current conversation |
| **Left panel rail** | Globe button next to "Plugins" / "Automated tasks" | Full-screen use, on demand |
| **Main area panel** | The full-screen panel both entries open | Immersive, left rail still usable |

All three **share one page instance**: scroll somewhere in the tab, switch to the panel and back — the page is exactly where you left it, still signed in, half-typed message intact.

---

## Why this one

Most sibling plugins offer a single global panel. What is different here:

| Aspect | dsh-webview | Typical embedded approach |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Conversation view tab entry | ✅ Yes (not seen elsewhere) | ❌ Global button only |
| Multiple entries, one instance | ✅ Three entries, one guest, never unmounted | ⚠️ Rare |
| Host-side code | ✅ None (`lib/index.js` is an empty plugin, just so the loader sees it) | ⚠️ Usually needed, to move HttpOnly cookies |
| Page permissions | ✅ Minimal: no file access, no credentials, no requests | ⚠️ Implementation dependent |
| Navigation scope | ✅ `deepseek.com` allowlist, everything else to the system browser | ⚠️ Often unrestricted |
| Size / dependencies | ✅ ~22 KB single file, zero runtime dependencies, no build step | ⚠️ Often hundreds of KB |

**No requests on your behalf**: no injected page scripts, no polling, no preloading, no proxying. The plugin does one thing — it puts the web app into a controlled embedded browser.

---

## Install

The desktop app ships its own package manager — **no Node.js required**:

```bash
dsh plugin --profile desktop add dsh-webview
```

Restart DSH: a globe appears in the left panel rail and a 网页端 tab in the conversation view bar.

Offline install from a local tarball:

```bash
dsh plugin --profile desktop add "C:\path\to\dsh-webview-0.3.0.tgz"
```

---

## Usage

1. Open either entry — the 网页端 tab in the conversation view bar, or the globe in the left rail
2. Log in to DeepSeek inside the panel the first time (a restart usually keeps you signed in)
3. The top bar offers **Refresh** and **Open in browser**

**About the "unusual environment" notice**: DSH Desktop is an Electron container, and DeepSeek's anti-abuse logic can tell it is not a regular browser. This plugin strips only the `Electron/<version>` token from the user agent and keeps every other Chromium marker — it does not impersonate another browser and does not fake a version. The notice normally no longer appears; if it does, just dismiss it — login and chat still work.

---

## How the session survives a restart

The host grants the embedded page an **in-memory partition** (random name, no `persist:` prefix, wiped when the process exits), so the plugin carries the session itself:

- On page load, panel switch and window close, it reads the page's `localStorage` and script-readable cookies into the DSH shell's `localStorage`
- On the next launch it writes that snapshot back **before** the page's own scripts initialise, reloads once when anything changed, and covers the surface meanwhile so no login page flashes

The snapshot is the same kind of thing a browser keeps to stay signed in, and it **never leaves your machine** — nothing is uploaded or sent anywhere. `HttpOnly` cookies cannot be read from a page, so an occasional re-login is still possible.

---

## Known limitations

- **Desktop only** — the web build of DSH has no Electron host; the surface shows a fallback notice
- **Restricted page permissions** — camera, microphone, clipboard read and downloads are disabled by the host policy
- **Needs an open conversation** — with no conversation open the view tab bar is not rendered; use the left-rail globe instead
- **Fixed position** — the button sits at the end of the left panel rail

---

## Compatibility

| DSH version | Status |
| --- | --- |
| 0.2.1-rc.1+ (desktop) | Verified |
| Web build of DSH | Unsupported (fallback notice) |

The plugin depends only on public client slots (`sidebar.panellist` / `main` / `conversation.view`) and the host `dshDesktop.browser` bridge. Minor 0.2.x updates generally need no change; a major update may need a small adaptation.

---

## How it works

- Registers three slots: `sidebar.panellist` (left rail button), `main` (panel body), `conversation.view` (conversation tab); all three share a single `<webview>` instance
- Acquires a lease and in-memory partition via `window.dshDesktop.browser.acquire()`, then creates the `<webview>`
- Keep-alive: the `<webview>` lives in a singleton container attached to `document.body`; switching entries only toggles `display`, so the page never reloads
- Navigation allowlist is `deepseek.com`; anything else goes to the system browser (deduplicated within 2 seconds)
- User agent: `webview.setUserAgent()` drops `Electron/x.y.z` only, keeping the rest of the Chromium markers

---

## Uninstall

```bash
dsh plugin --profile desktop remove dsh-webview
```

## Development

```
lib/index.js     host half (empty plugin, makes the loader entry visible)
lib/client.js    browser half (slots + webview), loaded via window.__ModuleLoader__
cordis.patch.yml bundle patch inserting the `webview` entry
```

